1.1.text:
Article 18

1. The Regional Government in efforts to protect women is responsible for:
a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women;
b. making efforts to prevent acts of violence against women including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;
c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women victims of violence;
d. increasing efforts to protect women workers;
e. strengthening and developing institutions providing women's protection services;
f. cooperating with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, business world and/or community non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent acts of violence against women; and
g. involving the active role of families, customary leaders, religious leaders, communities and community social institutions in the field of women's protection.
2. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by the Regional Apparatus implementing Government Affairs in the field of women's and children's empowerment and in its implementation may coordinate with related agencies.

Article 19

In order to prevent acts of violence against women, the Regional Government is obliged to:
a. conduct socialization regarding the prevention of violence against women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. provide guidelines on handling acts of violence against women;
c. involve community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. develop an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against women;
e. strengthen the function of community organizations engaged in women's and children's empowerment; and
f. provide public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women.

Article 20

1. Protection of women victims of violence is carried out through integrated services.
2. Provision of integrated services for women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) uses the principles of:
a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy; and
l. fulfillment of women's rights.

Article 22

Integrated complaint handling services as referred to in Article 21 letter a are carried out by:
a. hospitals;
b. community health centers;
c. women's service units;
d. trauma center shelters;
e. women's crisis centers;
f. marriage counseling, guidance and preservation advisory boards;
g. police;
h. prosecutor's office;
i. courts;
j. ministry of religion; and/or
k. legal aid institutions.
1. Complaints of acts of violence against women may be made by reporters consisting of victims, families, communities and/or community organizations in the field of women's and children's protection.
2. Reporters as referred to in paragraph (1) may file complaints:
a. directly;
b. by telephone; and/or
c. by letter.
3. In addition to handling complaints submitted by reporters, officers at service institutions as referred to in Article 22 are also authorized to handle victims of acts of violence against women obtained through other information.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for complaint handling services as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) are regulated in a Regent Regulation.

Article 4

The rights of children as referred to in Article 2 include:
a. obtaining education and teaching according to age level, physical and mental condition, intelligence and interests and talents;
b. living, growing, developing, and participating reasonably in accordance with human dignity and receiving protection from acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination and neglect, cruelty, violence and abuse, injustice and mistreatment;
c. having needs fulfilled by the family;
d. obtaining religious guidance;
e. obtaining their identity;
f. obtaining other rights in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
g. specifically for children with disabilities, the right to obtain facilities and special treatment in their services;

Article 5

In addition to the rights of women and children as referred to in Article 3 and Article 4, women and children victims of violence have the right to:
a. have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to complaints and services that are fast and affordable;
c. obtain information and legal certainty;
d. confidentiality of the case being handled;
e. compensation for the case being handled;
f. obtain facilities in the judicial process;
g. obtain assistance;
h. obtain physical and psychological health recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
i. determine their own decisions;
j. obtain rehabilitation and social reintegration;
k. obtain respect and services for survival, growth and development;
l. obtain basic services;
m. be free from negative stigma; dan
n. obtain freedom to determine choices to decide for themselves after their parents' divorce;

CONCERNING  WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

I. GENERAL

The 1945 Constitution as a constitutional foundation has explicitly regulated the importance of protection of Human Rights, including the rights of women and children. In addition to the guarantee from the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, the guarantee that every person must have equal dignity and degree has also been recognized internationally. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights or UDHR of 1948 which underlies Human Rights in general has guaranteed that all people are born with freedom, having equal dignity and rights without exception.

The 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia mandates the Regional Government to regulate and administer regional government affairs aimed at realizing the welfare of the community, through improving services, protection and empowerment of the community. As a component of society that plays an important role in realizing a quality generation and as successors to the nation's struggle, women and children must receive guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights.

To ensure the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection in accordance with human dignity and in order to carry out mandatory affairs, the regional government needs to provide legal certainty in the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection through the formulation of a policy. The formulation of such policy is a form of support and concrete effort from various parties, starting from the Regional Government, the community and other parties in ensuring the fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

The implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection is carried out based on the principles of:

a. non-discrimination:

b. the best interests of women and children;

c. the right to life, survival, and development;

d. respect for the views of the child; e. legal certainty;

f. local wisdom; and justice and gender equality.

The formulation of the Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to:

a. provide guidance in planning and implementing Women's Empowerment and Child Protection policies;

b. increase efforts for Women's Empowerment and fulfillment of children's rights in order to realize improved quality of life for women and children in the Region;

c. increase efforts to protect women and children from actions and policies that violate the rights of women and children;

d. increase the value of local wisdom and the role of custom in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and

e. increase the role of the Regional Government and other community institutions in efforts for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

This Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children regulates:

a. Improving the quality of life of women which contains government responsibility, implementation of gender mainstreaming, efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the political, legal, social and economic fields;

b. Protection of women which contains the responsibility of the regional government in the protection of women, prevention of violence against women, protection for women victims of violence, and handling of complaints;

c. Protection of children which contains the responsibility of the regional government, parents/family and the community in the protection of children and efforts made by the regional government in implementing child protection. In addition, the need to establish a children's forum and Child-Friendly Regency as a form of appreciation in realizing protection and fulfillment of children's rights.

Article 26

1. Counseling services as referred to in Article 25 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:
a. assist women victims of violence in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them themselves;
b. empower women victims of violence to determine their future;
c. provide moral support to women victims of violence in facing the process they are undergoing; and
d. make women victims of violence feel accepted in the community environment.
2. Counseling services are carried out by social rehabilitation officers who are gender responsive, consisting of:
a. counselors;
b. psychologists; and
c. psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women victims of violence.
4. The stages of counseling services include:
a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case discussion; and
d. site visits.

Article 30

1. Law enforcement and legal aid services are aimed at providing assistance and protection to women victims of violence and witnesses with a gender responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.
2. Types of legal aid services include:
a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of investigation reports;
b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal aid in the examination process at court;
d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and
e. legal aid in the mediation process.
3. The Regional Government provides legal aid to women victims of violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Article 58

1. Regional Government is obligated to provide social welfare for:a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of kidnapping and child victims of human trafficking;
c. children who do not have parents and are cared for by parties or families who are unable;
d. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters; ande. children with disabilities;
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services:a. health;
b. education;
c. legal aid;
d. assistance;
e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation; and/org. reintegration.

CHAPTER VIII

GENDER AND CHILD DATA SYSTEM

Article 69

The Regional Government, in order to provide protection to children:

a. prevents violence against children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;
b. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling on child protection and rights;
c. develops child-friendly school models;
d. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for children;
e. compiles databases and information on acts of violence against children;
f. maps locations and areas prone to violence against children;
g. coordinates and cooperates in preventing violations of children's rights;
h. strengthens the function of institutions or organizations engaged in child protection; and

i. encourages public awareness of the importance of child protection by establishing women and child protection assistants.

Service efforts undertaken by the Regional Government for women victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 20 through Article 33 shall apply mutatis mutandis to service efforts for women victims of acts of violence.

