CONCERNING  WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

I. GENERAL

The 1945 Constitution as a constitutional foundation has explicitly regulated the importance of protection of Human Rights, including the rights of women and children. In addition to the guarantee from the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, the guarantee that every person must have equal dignity and degree has also been recognized internationally. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights or UDHR of 1948 which underlies Human Rights in general has guaranteed that all people are born with freedom, having equal dignity and rights without exception.

The 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia mandates the Regional Government to regulate and administer regional government affairs aimed at realizing the welfare of the community, through improving services, protection and empowerment of the community. As a component of society that plays an important role in realizing a quality generation and as successors to the nation's struggle, women and children must receive guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights.

To ensure the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection in accordance with human dignity and in order to carry out mandatory affairs, the regional government needs to provide legal certainty in the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection through the formulation of a policy. The formulation of such policy is a form of support and concrete effort from various parties, starting from the Regional Government, the community and other parties in ensuring the fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

The implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection is carried out based on the principles of:

a. non-discrimination:

b. the best interests of women and children;

c. the right to life, survival, and development;

d. respect for the views of the child; e. legal certainty;

f. local wisdom; and justice and gender equality.

The formulation of the Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection aims to:

a. provide guidance in planning and implementing Women's Empowerment and Child Protection policies;

b. increase efforts for Women's Empowerment and fulfillment of children's rights in order to realize improved quality of life for women and children in the Region;

c. increase efforts to protect women and children from actions and policies that violate the rights of women and children;

d. increase the value of local wisdom and the role of custom in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and

e. increase the role of the Regional Government and other community institutions in efforts for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.

This Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children regulates:

a. Improving the quality of life of women which contains government responsibility, implementation of gender mainstreaming, efforts to increase the role, quality and capacity of women in the political, legal, social and economic fields;

b. Protection of women which contains the responsibility of the regional government in the protection of women, prevention of violence against women, protection for women victims of violence, and handling of complaints;

c. Protection of children which contains the responsibility of the regional government, parents/family and the community in the protection of children and efforts made by the regional government in implementing child protection. In addition, the need to establish a children's forum and Child-Friendly Regency as a form of appreciation in realizing protection and fulfillment of children's rights.

