Article 69

The Regional Government, in order to provide protection to children:

a. prevents violence against children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;
b. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling on child protection and rights;
c. develops child-friendly school models;
d. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for children;
e. compiles databases and information on acts of violence against children;
f. maps locations and areas prone to violence against children;
g. coordinates and cooperates in preventing violations of children's rights;
h. strengthens the function of institutions or organizations engaged in child protection; and

i. encourages public awareness of the importance of child protection by establishing women and child protection assistants.

Service efforts undertaken by the Regional Government for women victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 20 through Article 33 shall apply mutatis mutandis to service efforts for women victims of acts of violence.

Article 6

The implementation of women's empowerment and child protection is based on the principles of:
a. the best interests of women and children;
b. gender justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom;
d. law enforcement and legal certainty;
e. non-discrimination;
f. the right to life, survival, and development;
g. respect for the views of children; and

Article 20

1. Protection of women victims of violence is carried out through integrated services.
2. Provision of integrated services for women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) uses the principles of:
a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy; and
l. fulfillment of women's rights.

Article 63

1. Facilitation of child justice system includes criminal and civil justice.
2. Facilitation of criminal justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:a. provision of companion or child volunteer during examination by investigator;
b. provision of legal aid and psychologist services;
c. involving members/local police investigators in efforts to prevent criminal acts committed by Children, children as victims and children as witnesses;
d. realizing restorative justice process for children in conflict with the law; and
e. rehabilitation and social reintegration services;
3. Fulfillment of children's rights in the civil justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes provision and delivery of consultation and legal aid services during the judicial process.
4. In facilitating the child justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), the authorized PD is the PD that administers governmental affairs in the field of protection of women and children as well as social affairs.

Article 74

1. Parents in efforts to protect children's rights shall play a role in:
a. caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting children;
b. developing children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. providing formal and non-formal education;
d. guiding children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and
e. preventing early marriage.
2. In the event that parents are absent, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to any reason, are unable to carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

