Article 66

1. The Regional Action Plan for the Development of a Child-Friendly Regency is directed towards the fulfillment of children's rights divided into 5 (five) target groups, comprising:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, leisure time utilization, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.
2. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan for the Development of a Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated in a Regent Regulation.

1. In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of the Regional Action Plan for the Development of a Child-Friendly Regency in the Region, a Child-Friendly Regency Task Force shall be established.
2. The establishment and membership composition of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force shall be determined by a Regent's Decree.
3. The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force shall be domiciled at the OPD that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
4. The Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) shall have the following principal duties:
a. coordinating the implementation of policies and development of a Child-Friendly Regency;
b. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication, information and education on Child-Friendly Regency policies;
c. collecting baseline data for the development of a Child-Friendly Regency;
d. conducting needs analysis based on baseline data;
e. disseminating baseline data;
f. determining the focus and priorities of programs in realizing a Child-Friendly Regency, adjusted to regional potential;
g. preparing a 5 (five) year regional action plan for a Child-Friendly Regency and working mechanisms; and
h. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once (1) a year.

Article 63

1. Facilitation of child justice system includes criminal and civil justice.
2. Facilitation of criminal justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:a. provision of companion or child volunteer during examination by investigator;
b. provision of legal aid and psychologist services;
c. involving members/local police investigators in efforts to prevent criminal acts committed by Children, children as victims and children as witnesses;
d. realizing restorative justice process for children in conflict with the law; and
e. rehabilitation and social reintegration services;
3. Fulfillment of children's rights in the civil justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes provision and delivery of consultation and legal aid services during the judicial process.
4. In facilitating the child justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), the authorized PD is the PD that administers governmental affairs in the field of protection of women and children as well as social affairs.

